":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Then the LORD said to me, “Go show love to your wife again, though she is loved by another and is an adulteress. Love her as the LORD loves the Israelites, though they turn to other gods and love to offer raisin cakes to idols.”","verse":1},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Afterward, the people of Israel will return and seek the LORD their God and David their king. They will come trembling to the LORD and to His goodness in the last days.","verse":5}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Hos.1.2-3.5","r":"Hos.1.2-3.5","rawR":"Hos.1.2-3.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hos.1.2-3.5","value":"Hos.1.2-3.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:2–3:5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This section describes a small segment of the prophet’s life, focusing on his unhappy marriage and illuminating God’s painful relationship with Israel, his chosen people."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Hos.1.2-3.5","refs":[{"book":"HOS","endChapter":3,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Hos.1.2-3.5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"HOS","sourceChapterCoverage":[1,2,3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"fae56bbeb43f6e4e0a21d3d3d99a4d66ca3c5d340c3b67f9acbe9d06244c53d0","sourceItemName":"Hos.1.2-3.5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":10855,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"HOS","canonicalOSIS":"Hos","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Hos"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Hos.1.2-3.5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Hos.1.2-3.5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Hos.3.1","r":"Hos.3.1","rawR":"Hos.3.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hos.3.1","value":"Hos.3.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Go and love your wife again, even though she:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The Lord commanded Hosea to restore his marriage as testimony that the Lord had promised to restore wayward Israel. • ",{"c":["love to worship them:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Literally ",{"c":["love their raisin cakes."],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," The worship of Asherah, a Canaanite fertility goddess, often included offerings of raisin cakes."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Hos.3.1","refs":[{"book":"HOS","endChapter":3,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Hos.3.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"HOS","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"74214d6a8d2c0dc2643b01dd170fbf802d5c8bc8a510f09b501e395e6209eeb2","sourceItemName":"Hos.3.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":10881,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"HOS","canonicalOSIS":"Hos","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Hos"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Hos.3.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Hos.3.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Hos.3.2","r":"Hos.3.2","rawR":"Hos.3.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hos.3.2","value":"Hos.3.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["So I bought her back:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The biblical text does not indicate why the prophet needed to purchase his wife. Most interpreters have assumed that the unfaithful Gomer had fallen into debt and become a slave. The meager price Hosea paid for her may indicate that Gomer was considered a slave of little value (see ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.21.32","r":"Exod.21.32","rawR":"Exod.21.32","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.21.32","value":"Exod.21.32"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 21:32"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.27.4","r":"Lev.27.4","rawR":"Lev.27.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.27.4","value":"Lev.27.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 27:4"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Hos.3.2","refs":[{"book":"HOS","endChapter":3,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Hos.3.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"HOS","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0f260f23a37d2864ef6df5d56bb3e966dc14aa926e97f20fbf796c91ba48715b","sourceItemName":"Hos.3.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":10882,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"HOS","canonicalOSIS":"Hos","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Hos"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Hos.3.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Hos.3.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationSta